# Local-only.  The rule for everything below is the same: it is either not
# ours to publish, or it is a cache that can be rebuilt, or it is machine state
# that would only ever cause a conflict.

# --- 6.0 GB of whisper model cache.  Rebuildable, and no repo wants it. ---
_tools/models/

# --- ripped game assets: 214 MB + a 212 MB zip.  Not ours to publish, and the
#     zip is over GitHub's 100 MB hard file limit anyway. ---
TRG Sounds & Images pack/
TRG Sounds & Images pack.zip

# --- 104 MB of third-party YouTube guides, downloaded only so they could be
#     transcribed locally.  Copyrighted, and they are not part of the project. ---
Videos/

# --- paste / inbox / vision caches from the local vision bridge ---
.ai/

# --- the Studio <-> disk byte channel's scratch space.  _pull/ holds copies
#     PULLED OUT of Studio to be compared against src/; they are duplicates by
#     construction, so committing them would create a second copy of every
#     module that can drift from the one that actually runs.  The comparison is
#     the product, not the copies. ---
_tools/_pull/

# --- server logs from _tools/receive.py and _tools/serve.py.  Machine state on
#     a localhost port: it records when THIS session pulled what, which is not a
#     fact about the project and would conflict on every machine. ---
_tools/_attic/logs/

# --- the 8765 sink's own two streams.  It runs DETACHED so that the memory
#     reaper in run_tests.sh cannot take it the way it took 8766, and a detached
#     process has no console -- hence files here rather than under logs/.  Same
#     rule as above: a PID and a port on this machine, not a fact about the
#     project. ---
_tools/_attic/sink8765.log
_tools/_attic/sink8765.err.log

# --- machine-local claude state (the shared settings.json, if one is ever
#     added, is deliberately NOT ignored) ---
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# --- OS and editor cruft ---
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.tmp

# --- the original-game watcher's per-run instance dump.  Same rule as the room
#     watcher's, and NOT the same path shape: that place's roots sit one level
#     under Data/roomwatch_runN/, this one's sit under Data/originalwatch/<run>/,
#     so the pattern needs the extra level or it silently matches nothing -- which
#     is how the first version of this rule let an 11,684,084-byte file through.
#     The reason is the room watcher's reason: nothing reads it back (no script,
#     no test, no harness), the derived answers rooms.txt / summary.txt ARE
#     tracked, and it is regenerated by running the watcher again.  What I cannot
#     say here that I can say for the room watcher is byte-identity across runs --
#     there is ONE original-game run on disk, so identity is untested.  The leg
#     that carries without it is "nothing reads it". ---
Data/originalwatch*/**/inventory.txt

# --- the watch harness's FIXTURE run.  Not a recording of anything, and the
#     three things that say so are all inside it: rooms.txt's roots are LateA /
#     LateB / Repeated / PreLeverGone, the audio ids are rbxassetid://111..444,
#     and health.txt's own sink_last_error names watch_harness.luau:400.  How it
#     came to sit under _tools/ rather than under Data/ I cannot show from the
#     files -- the sink writes to originalwatch/<run>/ whatever its --dir is, and
#     the name here is _probe, so something moved them after the fact.  What is
#     certain is what matters: it is harness output, it is regenerated by running
#     the harness again, and a fresh suite run today leaves nothing behind, so it
#     gets the same treatment as _harness_out/ below. ---
_tools/_probe/

# --- the room watcher's per-run instance dump (Data/roomwatch*/inventory.txt).
#     Every run's copy is BYTE-IDENTICAL to every other run's -- all seven so
#     far, md5 5ae9ab97526f39b3fbe530d12f97f6f3, 5278228 bytes -- because it is
#     a snapshot of a world nobody edited between runs.  It is 5.3 MB a copy and
#     seven copies is 37 MB of the same file.  Nothing downstream reads it:
#     rooms.txt (5.7 KB) is the derived answer and it IS tracked, and the dump
#     can be regenerated by running the watcher again. ---
Data/roomwatch*/inventory.txt

# --- the watch harness's own output.  _harness_out/ is what --dump writes for a
#     human to read; _mut_base/ and _mut_out/ are the paired dumps the mutation
#     suite diffs against each other.  All three are regenerated on every run and
#     all three are deleted by the suite on the way out -- ignored as well so that
#     a run killed halfway leaves nothing staged. ---
_tools/_harness_out/
_tools/_mut_base/
_tools/_mut_out/
_tools/_watch_mutant.luau
# Same reasoning for the Python side: __pycache__ is written by the interpreter,
# not by me, and is regenerated on every run.  The two mutant files below are the
# other half of the same rule -- each suite deletes its own on the way out, and a
# run killed halfway would otherwise leave a deliberately broken copy of a shipped
# file sitting in the tree where `git add -A` would pick it up.
__pycache__/
_tools/_recorder_mutant.luau
_tools/_gui_mutant_states.luau

# --- the user's own auxiliary reference dumps (Data/auxcollection/).  Handed to
#     me to read, not produced by the project: 180 MB in ONE file, which is over
#     GitHub's 100 MB hard file limit, so an accidental `git add -A` would be
#     rejected on push rather than merely bloating the repo.  The FINDINGS live
#     in PROGRESS.md Phase 66 and in the three analysers under
#     _tools/_attic/scratch/; the raw dump is not needed to reproduce them. ---
Data/auxcollection/

# --- the Studio -> disk landing zone for the code-of-record refresh.  Files
#     arrive here over the localhost sink (see _tools/receive.py) and are then
#     verified by CRC-32 against the manifest Studio pushed alongside them, and
#     only THEN copied into src/.  This is a staging area, so it is scratch: the
#     authority is src/ (git-backed), never this.  Keeping it is useful -- it is
#     the evidence that the copy into src/ was byte-exact. ---
Data/reflect/

# --- the third-party transport probes.  These are the "is this channel alive at
#     all" files: five of them TOTAL 50 BYTES (the largest is 19), and every one
#     of them is a literal handshake string -- hello-from-studio, plugin-alive,
#     probe-ok-2026-10-02, x.  Nothing reads them back: no script, no test, no
#     harness.  Each was written once to prove a sink or a VM was reachable and
#     then left behind.  Machine state about a localhost port, same rule as
#     _tools/_attic/logs/ above -- and the same reason the file-shaped probe
#     scratch is listed here while the DIRECTORY-shaped _tools/_probe/ is listed
#     further up: the pattern has to match the shape that actually exists. ---
Data/__probe_post.txt
Data/_plugintest.txt
Data/_probe2.txt
_tools/_probe.txt

# --- Data/_transfer/ is the 8765 sink's landing zone, so it is scratch by
#     construction (the authority is wherever the bytes get copied TO, never the
#     place they land first).  Three of its four files are the handshake scratch
#     above, but the fourth is a 2,543,006-byte hgchamber_place.rbxl -- a whole
#     place export, pulled while I was chasing the wrong room.  HG Chamber turned
#     out to be the HOLOGRAPHIC chamber rather than the reactor chamber, and the
#     user's own words were that we are not using it for now.  So the export is
#     neither ours to publish nor needed to reproduce anything, and at 2.5 MB it
#     is well past the size where "just commit it" is free.  Nothing reads it. ---
Data/_transfer/

# --- the MCP build-library's own output directory.  generate_build / create_build
#     write here AUTOMATICALLY on every call, which is the whole reason to leave
#     it out: it is a tool's write target, not an authored asset, and a file
#     sitting there says nothing about whether anyone meant to keep it.  Its
#     single file today is misc/axis_probe_contour.json, a 4,985-byte contour
#     exported by a throwaway probe build.  A build worth keeping belongs under
#     src/ or Data/ with its existence stated in PROGRESS -- not left in a
#     directory the tool rewrites. ---
build-library/
